PYTHON LIBRARIES THAT CARRY NATIVE LIBRARIES OF THEIR OWN
=========================================================

  Every Python package the installer carries keeps its licence in its own dist-info
  folder. One carries libraries under licences that travel with the binary, so they are
  named here:

  pillow-heif             1.8.0, BSD-3-Clause for its own source. Reads a HEIF
                          photograph whole (kernel/heif.py), in a process of its own.
                          The installer carries a wheel built by
                          scripts/build_pillow_heif.bat rather than the published one,
                          so it has no HEVC encoder in it: Sift reads HEIF and never
                          writes it. The wheel carries:
                            libheif 1.23.4 (LGPL-3.0), built without x265. Its
                              complete source ships as
                              vendor/bin/sources/libheif-1.23.4.tar.gz.
                            libde265 1.1.3 (LGPL-3.0). Its complete source ships as
                              vendor/bin/sources/libde265-1.1.3.tar.gz.
                            the Microsoft C++ runtime (msvcp140), under the Visual
                              Studio redistribution terms.
                          The recipe that built them ships beside the sources as
                          vendor/bin/sources/build_pillow_heif.bat. Each library is a
                          DLL of its own, so it can be replaced by another build of it.
                          The published wheel (on PyPI, used for development) also
                          carries x265 (GPL-2.0-or-later) and the MinGW-w64 runtime;
                          the installer carries neither.

The versions above are the ones the installer ships, and they are pinned: the Python
packages in uv.lock, and the tools, the library sources and the pillow-heif wheel above by
SHA-256 in scripts/vendor_manifest.json. A
build's complete bill of materials is produced by scripts/sbom.sh.

If you build your own installer, keep this file accurate: swapping a bundled tool for one
with a different license changes the obligations that travel with it.
